1:# taskboard
2:
3:A keyboard-first task board for the terminal, built with [Textual](https://textual.textualize.io/).
4:Run it in a borderless terminal window, pin it on top, and it works as a desktop widget: ten
5:views over one JSON board, with optional team sync through a shared folder.
6:
7:<p align="center">
8:  <img src="docs/taskboard-ambient.gif" width="760" alt="taskboard — the lanes view, live"><br>
9:  <img src="docs/taskboard-gantt.svg" width="380" alt="gantt — the whole board on a window fitted to the open work">
10:  <img src="docs/taskboard-kanban.png" width="380" alt="kanban — every task in its phase column"><br>
11:  <img src="docs/taskboard-agenda.png" width="380" alt="agenda — every due date on one shared axis">
12:  <img src="docs/taskboard-lanes.png" width="380" alt="lanes — one panel per project"><br>
13:  <sub>Renders of the seeded demo board. The gantt is current; the other images predate this
14:  release's colour changes.</sub>
15:</p>
16:
17:## What it is
18:
19:- **A widget, not a window.** No borders: every view commits with rules, and it reflows to
20:  whatever size the terminal gives it.
21:- **Ten views** over the same tasks — lanes, agenda, gantt, kanban, focus, chains, flow, standup,
22:  people, setup — each one key away.
23:- **One file.** Your board is a single JSON file in your home directory; nothing goes to a
24:  server.
25:- **Optional team mode.** Point it at a shared folder and each person's board syncs there;
26:  teammates' cards show up read-only.
27:
28:## Requirements
29:
30:- Python **3.12 or newer** (`requires-python = ">=3.12"`).
31:- **Windows first.** It is built and used on Windows; opening raw image files uses
32:  `os.startfile`, which is Windows-only. The clipboard code has macOS and Linux branches, but
33:  those platforms are not tested here.
34:- Built and verified against **Textual 8.2.8 / rich 15.0.0** (pinned in `requirements.txt`).
35:  The install pulls in every runtime dependency (`textual`, `tzdata`, `pillow`,
36:  `textual-image`).
37:
38:## Install
39:
40:Clone the repository, then install it as a command.
41:
42:```powershell
43:git clone https://github.com/jav201/taskboard.git
44:cd taskboard
45:```
46:
47:**With pipx (recommended).** pipx gives the app its own environment and puts `taskboard` on
48:your PATH:
49:
50:```powershell
51:python -m pip install --user pipx
52:python -m pipx ensurepath        # then open a new terminal so PATH updates
53:pipx install .
54:```
55:
56:To update after pulling new code: `pipx reinstall taskboard`. This installs the newest
57:Textual; the exact versions the app is verified against are pinned in `requirements.txt`
58:(see [`RUN.md`](RUN.md)).
59:
60:**With pip.** `pip install --user .` installs the same `taskboard` command. If the shell says
61:it is not recognized, your Python user-scripts folder is not on PATH; this prints it:
62:
63:```powershell
64:python -c "import os, sysconfig; print(sysconfig.get_path('scripts', f'{os.name}_user'))"
65:```
66:
67:**From source.** See [`RUN.md`](RUN.md): a virtual environment and `python -m taskboard`.
68:
69:## Quick start
70:
71:```powershell
72:taskboard
73:```
74:
75:The first run creates your board and seeds it with demo data. Press `?` in any view for its
76:help — what the view is for, what its marks mean, an example and its keys; inside help, `m`
77:shows the full keymap and `?` opens the command palette. `q` quits.
78:
79:## Views
80:
81:| Key | View | One line |
82:|-----|------|----------|
83:| `1` | **Lanes** | One panel per project with its load and due work; `Tab` switches the grid (default) and the classic waves layout |
84:| `2` | **Agenda** | Every dated task on one shared day axis, by urgency |
85:| `3` | **Gantt** | The whole board on a window fitted to the open work, with a date ruler on top |
86:| `4` | **Kanban** | Every task in its phase column; `Tab` cycles grouped, matrix and lanes layouts |
87:| `5` | **Focus** | Pinned tasks and pinned projects, in five layouts (`Tab`) |
88:| `6` | **Chains** | The chain map: who waits on whom, per project — the ready/waits/late marks, the critical chain, and the per-chain `dates` rule on screen |
89:| `7` | **Flow** | Read-only: time in phase, a phase × week heatmap, weekly throughput |
90:| `8` | **Standup** | Team mode: one row per teammate — load, top task, sync age |
91:| `9` | **People** | Team mode: one lane per teammate; their cards are read-only |
92:| `0` | **Setup** | Team configuration, edited in the app with live health checks |
93:
94:**Gantt.** Every project has a row. The window fits the open work (from half a day to a week
95:per cell, the smallest that holds it); when rows run out, projects fold to one span row
96:(`▸ name  N open`) while the selected task's project stays open (`▾`). Finished work folds into
97:a `✓n` count. Each row ends in one due chip (`▲3d` late, `today`, `Oct 6`, `no due`), and `↳`
98:marks a task waiting on open work. The two rows on top are the ruler: months, day numbers,
99:and the selected task's exact start and due (`⟦━⟧`).
100:
101:## Keys
102:
103:The key bar at the bottom shows the keys that work in the current view; `;` switches it
104:between the short and the full layer. Every key below is also in the command palette.
105:
106:| Key | Name | One line |
107:|-----|------|----------|
108:| `?` | Help | Per-view help (usage, legend, example, keys); `m` there for the full map |
109:| `;` | More | Toggle the key bar's layer |
110:| `q` | Quit | Quit |
111:| `1`–`6`, `7`–`9`, `0` | Views | See [Views](#views) |
112:| `↑` `↓` / `k` `j` | Move | Move the selection in the order the view draws |
113:| `←` `→` / `h` `l` | Across | Move between columns (kanban) |
114:| `Enter` | Details | Every field of the selected task; its links can be changed there |
115:| `a` / `e` | Add / Edit | Add a task / edit the selected one in the full-screen editor |
116:| `d` / `Delete` | Delete | Delete the selected task (asks first) |
117:| `L` | Link | Make the selected task wait on another one (a picker; on the gantt, a link mode) |
118:| `M` | Milestone | Make the selected task a milestone — one date, its due — or a task again |
119:| `[` `]` | Phase | Move the selected task one phase back / forward |
120:| `!` | Priority | Cycle low → normal → high |
121:| `b` | Blocked | Mark an outside block (`▲`) on the task, or clear it |
122:| `+` `=` / `-` | Due | Due date one day later / earlier — what waits on it follows by the project's rule |
123:| `m` | Chain | Re-apply the last date move under the next mode (stay / push / together) |
124:| `u` | Undo | Undo the last quick change |
125:| `x` / `v` | Archive | Archive or unarchive / show archived tasks |
126:| `X` | Purge done | Archive all finished work that has no completion date (asks first) |
127:| `t` / `T` | Pin | Pin the task / its whole project (Focus view) |
128:| `o` / `i` | Links / Images | Open the task's http(s) links / view its images |
129:| `p` / `P` | Projects | Add a project / manage projects |
130:| `f` | Phases | Add, rename, reorder or delete the board's phases |
131:| `s` `g` `z` | Kanban | Sort, group, collapse the last phase |
132:| `F` / `/` / `esc` | Focus, filter | Kanban and gantt: focus one project / filter tasks / clear |
133:| `Tab` | Layout | Cycle the layouts of kanban, focus and lanes |
134:| `R` / `S` | Present / Standup | Present the selected task's project (gantt + brief, export SVG+PNG) / show the week's standup |
135:| `c` | Clocks | Choose the ribbon's two city clocks |
136:| `space` / `ctrl+s` | Setup | In Setup: toggle a control / save; `Enter` edits a row, `a` / `x` add or remove a row, `Tab` changes section, `esc` discards |
137:
138:In the task and project editors, `Ctrl+E` inserts an emoji and `Ctrl+V` pastes text.
139:
140:## Working with tasks
141:
142:- **Phases.** A new board has `Backlog`, `Doing`, `Done`; `f` edits them. The last phase means
143:  done.
144:- **Undo.** `u` reverts the last phase move, priority, blocked flag, due change, pin, archive
145:  or delete. Adding a task is not undoable.
146:- **Finished work.** A task 20 days in its done phase is archived at startup (archived, never
147:  deleted). `X` archives all finished work that has no completion date (asks first); `v` shows archived
148:  tasks and `x` brings one back.
149:- **Projects.** `p` adds one; `P` manages them (edit, archive, delete). Archiving a project
150:  archives its open tasks too; deleting a project moves its tasks to the Inbox.
151:
152:## Milestones
153:
154:A **milestone** is a task with one date — its due — and no duration: a commitment, not work.
155:`M` on the selected task makes it one (its start becomes its due) or a task again; the editor
156:(`e`) has the same `milestone` box. A task with no date cannot be a milestone. `+`/`-` move a
157:milestone's single date, `u` undoes, and team sync carries the flag.
158:
159:In the **gantt** a milestone is a row ` ◆ title` with a `◆` on its date and a chip saying how far
160:it is (`in 10d`, `today`, `▲2d` late); a reached one turns ash (`◆✓`, `✓ done`) and stays among its
161:project's rows while the project has open work. The ruler marks the selected project's milestones.
162:
163:In the **kanban** a milestone is never a card and never counted: it rides its project's band rule
164:(`◆ Oct 10 Launch · in 10d`), late ones first, then the upcoming ones, then the last one reached.
165:
166:**The one-time milestone offer.** The first time this version opens a board that holds one-day
167:tasks (start = due) or dated tasks with no start, it offers — once — to make milestones of them:
168:one-day tasks come pre-checked, the others unchecked; `space` toggles, `↵` converts exactly the
169:checked ones, `esc` converts none (and the offer does not come back). Converting first copies the
170:board file to `<board>.pre-milestones` and writes `<board>.milestones-log`; `u` undoes the whole
171:conversion. A board this version creates is never offered. Restoring the backup file brings back a
172:board without the offer's mark, so it is offered again at the next start.
173:
174:## Links and blocking
175:
176:A **link** says a task waits on another one. `L` on the selected task opens a picker: tasks of
177:the same project first, then by due date; type to filter; each row says what the link would mean
178:for the dates ("overlaps 3d", "ok — due 2d before this starts"). A task already linked says
179:`↵ removes`; one that would close a loop is shown with the loop and cannot be picked. The picker
180:can also create a new task to wait on. On the gantt, `L` opens a link mode that draws the link
181:before you make it. `u` undoes a link like any quick change.
182:
183:Cards show `◂N` when the task waits on N open tasks and `▸N` when N open tasks wait on it. When
184:the last task it waits on is finished, the board says once that it is ready. The details view
185:(`Enter`) lists what a task waits on and what it unblocks, down the chain, with any date
186:conflict; there `tab` moves to the links, `x` removes one, `↵` jumps to it and `L` adds one.
187:
188:`b` is the **outside block** (`▲`): something no task on the board stands for. It is independent
189:of links. A task that open tasks still wait on cannot be archived or deleted (nor its project
190:archived) until it is finished or the links are removed — the refusal names who waits.
191:
192:The kanban `unblock` sort puts tasks with the most waiting work first, and the gantt draws the
193:critical chain (the longest chain of open links) as a heavy `━` line.
194:
195:**The one-time link migration.** Boards written by earlier versions recorded a link every time
196:`b` blocked a task and kept it after unblocking. The first start of this version reads those
197:links once: a task still blocked on a task now waits on it, links that had been released are
198:dropped, and blocks with no task to point at stay. Before changing anything it copies the board
199:file to `<board file>.pre-links-migration` and writes every change to
200:`<board file>.links-migration-log`; `u` right after the start undoes it. It never runs again on
201:its own. To go back later, close the app and copy the backup over the board file — the restored
202:board is migrated again at the next start. If the backup cannot be written the app does not open
203:the board (free space or write access in its folder, or run `taskboard --board` on a copy).
204:
205:## Kanban
206:
207:- `s` cycles the sort: project → priority → due → recent → unblock.
208:- `g` cycles the grouping: project → priority → horizon.
209:- `z` collapses the last (done) phase to one count row.
210:- Open high-priority cards float to a `high` band at the top of each column (grouped layout),
211:  and every open card wears a priority badge: `!!` high, `==` normal, `++` low.
212:- Phase headers show a WIP limit as `count/limit`, red when over; the default is 3 for `Doing`
213:  (`wip_limits` in the board's settings).
214:
215:## Notes, links and images
216:
217:- In notes, `==text==`, `!!text!!` and `++text++` are highlighted yellow, red and green.
218:- A task with a URL shows `↗`; `o` opens its http(s) links in your browser.
219:- In the editor, **Paste image** saves the clipboard image as a real file; `i` views a task's
220:  images inline, and `o` inside the viewer opens them in your default app.
221:
222:## Team mode
223:
224:Team mode syncs boards through any shared folder — a synced drive or a git checkout works the
225:same. Turn it on in **Setup** (`0`): the shared folder, who you are, which projects are shared, the
226:roster, and a time in minutes after which a teammate whose board has not synced is shown as
227:stale. The app syncs every 30 minutes. The folder holds `team.json` (the roster and
228:shared projects) and one `board.<user>.json` per person. Teammates' cards appear read-only in
229:**Standup** (`8`) and **People** (`9`). Setup checks the folder as you edit and says what is
230:wrong.
231:
232:## Presentation
233:
234:`R` opens the presentation of the selected task's project (the focused project when set): the
235:gantt field on top, the brief blocks below, and a cursor whose task's notes expand (`←`/`→`
236:and `↑`/`↓` move it). `x` exports what it shows to an SVG and a PNG in the `reports` folder;
237:`esc` leaves. The presentation is read-only — it never writes the board.
238:
239:## Reports
240:
241:From the shell:
242:
243:```powershell
244:taskboard --report                       # the whole board
245:taskboard --report "Website Redesign"    # one project
246:```
247:
248:Each writes one self-contained HTML file to a `reports` folder beside your board file and
249:prints the path. It shows counts and dates only — no forecasts — and never modifies an
250:existing board (pointed at a board file that does not exist yet, it creates the demo board
251:first, as the app does). An unknown project name exits with code 2 and lists the projects.
252:
253:## Clocks
254:
255:The bottom ribbon shows the time, date, ISO week and two city clocks (default Mexico City and
256:New York). `c` changes them: type a city and it autocompletes. 340 cities are available,
257:daylight saving included (`zoneinfo`).
258:
259:## Frameless window (WezTerm)
260:
261:Windows Terminal always draws a title bar, so use [WezTerm](https://wezterm.org). This repo
262:ships a config, [`wezterm.lua`](wezterm.lua): no window decorations, no tab bar, a dark
263:background. Load it with `wezterm --config-file wezterm.lua`, or copy it to `~/.wezterm.lua`.
264:
265:- It opens your normal shell; uncomment `default_prog` in the file to boot straight into
266:  `taskboard`.
267:- `Ctrl+Shift+B` toggles the window frame and `F11` borderless fullscreen (WezTerm keys).
268:- Keep it on top with PowerToys **Always On Top** (`Win+Ctrl+T`).
269:
270:## Data files
271:
272:Everything lives under `~/.taskboard/` (or beside the file you pass with `--board`):
273:
274:| File | What |
275:|------|------|
276:| `board.json` | Your board: projects, tasks, settings |
277:| `board.json.corrupt` | A copy of an unreadable board, kept before the app starts empty |
278:| `history.jsonl` | One line per phase move and per added task — the Flow view reads it |
279:| `images/<task id>/` | Pasted images, as plain files |
280:| `reports/` | HTML reports and the presentation's SVG + PNG exports |
281:
282:Never commit a real board: the repo's pre-commit hook (`tools/precommit_privacy.py`) refuses
283:files that carry your board's text.
284:
285:## Command line
286:
287:| Command | What |
288:|---------|------|
289:| `taskboard` | Run the app on `~/.taskboard/board.json` |
290:| `taskboard --board PATH` | Run it on another board file |
291:| `taskboard --report [PROJECT]` | Write a report and exit |
292:
293:`python -m taskboard` is the same command.
294:
295:## Development
296:
297:See [`RUN.md`](RUN.md) for the environment, the test suite and the privacy hook. CI runs the
298:suite on pushes to `main` and on pull requests (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`).
299:
300:```
301:taskboard/
302:  __main__.py     command line
303:  app.py          the App: views, selection, keys, team sync timer
304:  keymap.py       every key binding (the one source of the key bar and the palette)
305:  views.py        the view renderers, nav order and per-view help
306:  modals.py       editors, pickers, help and other modal screens
307:  models.py       Project / Task / Board: JSON store, seeding, rescue
308:  history.py      the log of phase moves and added tasks (history.jsonl)
309:  team_sync.py    team mode: the shared folder's files, merge, health check
310:  report.py       the HTML report
311:  ribbon.py       the bottom clock ribbon
312:  wave.py         the lanes waves engine
313:  taskboard.tcss  styles
314:tests/            pytest suite
315:tools/            privacy sweep and pre-commit check
316:docs/             screenshots and a sample report
317:wezterm.lua       frameless WezTerm config
318:```
319:
320:## Colours
321:
322:Eight project colours (lime, green, sky, blue, indigo, violet, fuchsia, pink); red, amber and
323:teal are kept for overdue, for due today, and for today and the filter field. Boards saved with a retired colour are remapped
324:once on load.
325:
326:## History
327:
328:- **Columns was retired**: the kanban is the same phase grid and loses nothing, so the views
329:  were renumbered.
330:- Key `6` is free: it belonged to the aperture widget view, which was removed.
331:
332:## Limitations
333:
334:- Raw image opening is Windows-only; macOS and Linux are untested.
335:- The Standup and People views need team mode.
336:- A real board is never part of this repo; the screenshots use the seeded demo data.
